= Solution
{parent=b}

Hermiticity supplies the reverse <matrix elements>, also equal to the real number $t$. In an <orthonormal basis> for the degenerate <eigenspace>, the perturbation is
$$
H_1=t\begin{pmatrix}0&1&1\\1&0&1\\1&1&0\end{pmatrix}
=t(\mathbf J_3-I),
$$
where $\mathbf J_3$ is the <all-ones matrix>. Its symmetric <vector> $(1,1,1)/\sqrt3$ has <eigenvalue> $2t$. Every <vector> whose components sum to zero has <eigenvalue> $-t$; that subspace has dimension two. For example, $(1,-1,0)/\sqrt2$ and $(1,1,-2)/\sqrt6$ form an <orthonormal basis> for it.

The first-order energies are therefore
$$
\boxed{E_0+2t\quad\text{once},\qquad E_0-t\quad\text{twice}.}
$$
The <trace> of the shifts is $2t-t-t=0$, agreeing with the zero diagonal. The ordering depends on the sign of $t$; the formula does not assume that the symmetric state is the lowest. Diagonalizing the perturbation inside the degenerate space is essential: the original diagonal entries alone would incorrectly predict no splitting.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

Using a spherical Bohr-radius volume for the atom, the calculations below give a cutoff electromagnetic <vacuum energy density> of about $6.2\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$, compared with a <hydrogen> binding-energy density of about $3.5\times10^{12}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$. **The cutoff vacuum estimate is about $1.8\times10^3$ times larger.** Alternative reasonable definitions of atomic volume change this ratio by a factor of a few, not the main order-of-magnitude conclusion.

The cutoff result is a bare zero-point mode sum. It is not a classical radiation bath incident on the atom, nor a calculation of an observable absolute vacuum energy without a subtraction or <renormalization> prescription.

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In a large periodic quantization volume $\mathcal V$, one <wavevector> state occupies $(2\pi)^3/\mathcal V$ in <wavevector> space. There are two transverse <photon polarizations>, and each oscillator contributes $\hbar ck/2$. A lower <wavelength> cutoff $\lambda_{\min}$ corresponds to $k_{\max}=2\pi/\lambda_{\min}$, so the <energy density> is
$$
u_{\rm vac}
=2\int_{|\mathbf k|\leq k_{\max}}\frac{\mathrm d^3k}{(2\pi)^3}
\frac{\hbar ck}{2}
=\frac{\hbar c}{2\pi^2}\int_0^{k_{\max}}k^3\,\mathrm dk
=\frac{\hbar c\,k_{\max}^4}{8\pi^2}
=\frac{2\pi^2\hbar c}{\lambda_{\min}^4}.
$$
For $\lambda_{\min}=10^{-10}\,\mathrm m$,
$$
\boxed{u_{\rm vac}\simeq6.24\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}.}
$$
The factor two is <polarization>, not an extra independent count of positive and negative frequencies. The continuum <wavevector> integral already counts independent travelling-wave modes in the usual field quantization convention. The quartic cutoff dependence also shows why an assumed shortest <wavelength> is crucial.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take the <hydrogen> ground-state binding-energy magnitude as $E_B\simeq13.6\,\mathrm{eV}=2.18\times10^{-18}\,\mathrm J$, and use $a_0\simeq5.29\times10^{-11}\,\mathrm m$ as its characteristic radius. A definite volume convention is
$$
V_H=\frac{4\pi a_0^3}{3}\simeq6.21\times10^{-31}\,\mathrm{m^3}.
$$
It gives
$$
\boxed{u_H=\frac{E_B}{V_H}\simeq3.51\times10^{12}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}},
\qquad\frac{u_{\rm vac}}{u_H}\simeq1.78\times10^3.}
$$
A quantum atom has no sharp outer surface. If instead its characteristic volume is taken as $a_0^3$, the estimate is $1.47\times10^{13}\,\mathrm{J\,m^{-3}}$ and the ratio is about $4.2\times10^2$. Both conventions place the two densities roughly three orders of magnitude apart at order-of-magnitude accuracy. The comparison uses the magnitude of the <binding energy>, not its negative sign relative to <ionization>.

