= Solution
{parent=a}

**The <WKB approximation> describes a wave whose local <wavelength> changes slowly compared with one <wavelength>.** For the one-dimensional stationary <Schrödinger equation>, put $p(x)=\sqrt{2m[E-V(x)]}$. In a <classically allowed region>,
$$
\psi(x)\simeq\frac{C_+}{\sqrt{p(x)}}\exp\!\left[\frac{i}{\hbar}\int^x p(s)\,\mathrm ds\right]
+\frac{C_-}{\sqrt{p(x)}}\exp\!\left[-\frac{i}{\hbar}\int^x p(s)\,\mathrm ds\right].
$$
The <phase> is the classical action divided by $\hbar$. The amplitude varies as $p^{-1/2}$ so that a single travelling component has constant <probability current>. A useful local validity condition is $|\hbar p'/p^2|\ll1$.

For $V>E$, write $\kappa=\sqrt{2m(V-E)}/\hbar$. The wave instead has growing and decaying components proportional to $\kappa^{-1/2}\exp(\pm\int\kappa\,\mathrm dx)$. At a simple <turning point>, $p$ vanishes and neither local WKB expression is valid. Linearizing the potential gives an Airy-function solution that connects the two sides and supplies the turning-point <phase> shift. It is not legitimate to carry the divergent WKB amplitude straight through the <turning point>.<Topics in Quantum Mechanics by David Tong>[§2.2, semiclassical expansion and linear-potential matching]{ref}

**A useful application is tunnelling through a smooth opaque barrier.** With <turning points> $x_1,x_2$, the leading <transmission probability> is
$$
\boxed{T\simeq\exp\!\left[-2\int_{x_1}^{x_2}\kappa(x)\,\mathrm dx\right].}
$$
The exponent is twice the forbidden-region action in units of $\hbar$, because transmission is a <probability> rather than a wave amplitude. For an ideal rectangular barrier of height $V_0>E$ and width $d$, the same leading exponential is $\exp[-2d\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}/\hbar]$; matching at the abrupt edges supplies an additional prefactor.

\Image[paper-1-wkb-tunnelling.png]
{title=Original smooth-barrier construction and leading WKB transmission exponent; the shaded interval is classically forbidden}

For a bound state between two smooth <turning points>, the related WKB quantization rule is $\int_{x_1}^{x_2}p\,\mathrm dx=(n+1/2)\pi\hbar$. In a <harmonic potential> the integral equals $\pi E/\omega$, recovering $E_n=(n+1/2)\hbar\omega$. This exact agreement of energies for the harmonic oscillator does not make WKB <wavefunctions> uniformly exact near its <turning points>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

**<Stimulated emission> adds a <photon> to an already occupied radiation mode; <spontaneous emission> can occur when that mode initially contains no <photons>.** In the <electric-dipole approximation> the atom-field interaction is $H_{\rm int}=-\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf E$. Quantizing the radiation field makes the emission <matrix element> into a mode with occupation $n$ proportional to
$$
\langle n+1|a^\dagger|n\rangle=\sqrt{n+1}.
$$
This <photon occupation enhancement of emission> makes the emission rate into that mode proportional to $n+1$: the part proportional to $n$ is stimulated and the remaining part is spontaneous. Absorption is proportional to $|\langle n-1|a|n\rangle|^2=n$. The <photon> energy is the atomic energy difference, up to any specified recoil or line broadening.

The stimulated contribution preferentially populates the stimulating mode, including its propagation direction and <polarization>; it provides <optical gain>. Spontaneous <photons> populate the available modes according to the transition's dipole <matrix elements> and radiation environment. They are not universally isotropic. An excited bare atom with the field vacuum is not an <eigenstate> of the fully coupled <Hamiltonian>, which resolves the apparent paradox of a nominally stationary excited state radiating.<Topics in Quantum Mechanics by David Tong>[§4.3.2, why the electromagnetic field must be included to describe spontaneous decay]{ref}

\Image[paper-1-emission.png]
{title=Original two-level transition diagrams and the $n$ versus $n+1$ occupation factors for <photon> absorption and emission}

For lower and upper levels 1 and 2, with radiation spectral <energy density> $u_\nu$ per unit ordinary frequency, the rates are
$$
R_{\rm abs}=N_1B_{12}u_\nu,\qquad
R_{\rm stim}=N_2B_{21}u_\nu,\qquad
R_{\rm sp}=N_2A_{21}.
$$
Thermal detailed balance with the <Planck spectrum> gives
$$
\boxed{g_1B_{12}=g_2B_{21},\qquad
\frac{A_{21}}{B_{21}}=\frac{8\pi h\nu^3}{c^3}.}
$$
The statistical weights $g_i$ here are level degeneracies, not <Landé g-factors>. A definition of $B$ multiplying intensity or angular-frequency density would change the normalization factors.

For a specified free-space electric-dipole transition, summing radiation modes gives
$$
A_{21}=\frac{\omega_{21}^3|\mathbf d_{12}|^2}
{3\pi\epsilon_0\hbar c^3},
\qquad \omega_{21}=2\pi\nu.
$$
Appropriate sums over final magnetic substates and averages over initial substates are needed for an unresolved degenerate level. The radiative lifetime is $1/A_{21}$ only when this is the sole decay channel. In a <laser>, net stimulated gain requires $N_2/g_2>N_1/g_1$, as well as sufficient gain to overcome cavity loss; <population inversion> alone does not specify the lasing threshold.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**<Fermi's golden rule> converts a weak transition <matrix element> and the available density of final states into a transition rate.** For a static perturbation $W$ and a continuum,
$$
\boxed{\Gamma_{i\to{\rm all}}=\frac{2\pi}{\hbar}
\int |\langle f|W|i\rangle|^2\delta(E_f-E_i)\,\mathrm dN_f.}
$$
When the <matrix element> is nearly constant on the energy shell, this becomes $(2\pi/\hbar)|W_{fi}|^2\rho(E_i)$. The energy delta function is to be integrated, rather than interpreted as a finite rate to one exactly resonant discrete level. The observation time must resolve a smooth final-state continuum while remaining short enough that first-order depletion is small. A periodically driven perturbation instead gives the corresponding absorption and emission energy shells.

For elastic scattering from a weak localized potential $W(\mathbf r)$, use box-normalized free states $\langle\mathbf r|\mathbf k\rangle=\mathcal V^{-1/2}e^{i\mathbf k\cdot\mathbf r}$. Then
$$
W_{fi}=\frac1{\mathcal V}\widetilde W(\mathbf q),\qquad
\widetilde W(\mathbf q)=\int e^{-i\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf r}
W(\mathbf r)\,\mathrm d^3r,\qquad
\mathbf q=\mathbf k_f-\mathbf k_i.
$$
The number of final states is $\mathcal V k_f^2\,\mathrm dk_f\,\mathrm d\Omega/(2\pi)^3$. With $E_f=\hbar^2k_f^2/(2m)$, the density per energy and <solid angle> is $\mathcal V m k_f/[(2\pi)^3\hbar^2]$. Dividing the resulting golden-rule rate by the incident single-particle flux $j_i=\hbar k_i/(m\mathcal V)$ gives
$$
\frac{\mathrm d\sigma}{\mathrm d\Omega}
=\frac{m^2}{4\pi^2\hbar^4}\frac{k_f}{k_i}
|\widetilde W(\mathbf q)|^2.
$$
This is the <golden-rule derivation of the Born cross-section>. For the elastic problem $k_f=k_i$, so the first Born <scattering amplitude> and cross-section are
$$
\boxed{f_B(\mathbf k_f,\mathbf k_i)
=-\frac{m}{2\pi\hbar^2}\widetilde W(\mathbf q),\qquad
\frac{\mathrm d\sigma}{\mathrm d\Omega}=|f_B|^2.}
$$
The box volume cancels, as a physical cross-section must. The minus sign is fixed by the outgoing-wave scattering convention; the golden-rule rate alone determines only the modulus.

For example, $W(r)=g e^{-\kappa r}/r$ has $\widetilde W=4\pi g/(q^2+\kappa^2)$, hence $f_B=-2mg/[\hbar^2(q^2+\kappa^2)]$, with $q=2k\sin(\theta/2)$. Replacing the exact scattering state by the incident <plane wave> is the essential <Born approximation>. It can fail for strong scattering, low-energy resonances and situations requiring repeated scattering; a small perturbation and a suitable continuum are separate conditions.<Topics in Quantum Mechanics by David Tong>[§6.3, Born approximation and Yukawa-potential example]{ref}

= 3
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

**<Spin> and spatial exchange symmetry.** Write the normalized <spin> singlet and <triplet states> as
$$
\chi_{00}=\frac{|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle-|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle}{\sqrt2},
\qquad
\chi_{11}=|\uparrow\uparrow\rangle,\quad
\chi_{10}=\frac{|\uparrow\downarrow\rangle+|\downarrow\uparrow\rangle}{\sqrt2},
\quad\chi_{1,-1}=|\downarrow\downarrow\rangle.
$$
The singlet is antisymmetric under exchange and has total <spin> zero; the triplet is symmetric and has total <spin> one. For orthogonal orbitals define
$$
C=\frac{|ab\rangle+|ba\rangle}{\sqrt2},\qquad
A=\frac{|ab\rangle-|ba\rangle}{\sqrt2}.
$$
Identical <spin one-half> particles are <fermions>, so the complete state must be antisymmetric. Thus **$C\chi_{00}$ is allowed, and $A\chi_{1M}$ is allowed for each triplet component**. The spatially symmetric same-orbital states $|aa\rangle,|bb\rangle$ also require the singlet. A same-orbital triplet vanishes by the <Pauli exclusion principle>.

These products give a spin-adapted basis. More generally the physical state can be written
$$
|\Psi\rangle=(c_a|aa\rangle+c_b|bb\rangle+c_C C)\chi_{00}
+A\sum_{M=-1}^{1}d_M\chi_{1M}.
$$
A superposition of the singlet and triplet sectors need not factor into one spatial function times one <spin> function. Factorization holds for a definite-spin sector, as used in the subsequent model. If the original normalized orbitals are not orthogonal, the corresponding $ab\pm ba$ normalization is $\sqrt{2(1\pm|\langle a|b\rangle|^2)}$; the remainder of this calculation uses the stipulated orthogonal orbitals.

**Integral <matrix elements> and positivity.** Let $a(\mathbf r)$ and $b(\mathbf r)$ denote the localized <spatial orbitals>. With fixed <proton> positions, the one-electron electronic operator is
$$
h=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2m_e}\nabla^2
-\frac{e^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
\left(\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf R_a|}+
\frac1{|\mathbf r-\mathbf R_b|}\right).
$$
The proton-proton repulsion is a separate common nuclear energy. The requested one-electron and direct two-electron elements are
$$
\langle a|h|a\rangle=\int a^*(\mathbf r)\,h\,a(\mathbf r)\,\mathrm d^3r,
$$
$$
J_{ab}=\langle ab|H_{1,2}|ab\rangle
=\frac{e^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
\iint\frac{|a(\mathbf r_1)|^2|b(\mathbf r_2)|^2}
{|\mathbf r_1-\mathbf r_2|}\,\mathrm d^3r_1\,\mathrm d^3r_2.
$$
Since $H_1=h\otimes I$,
$$
\boxed{\langle aa|H_1|ab\rangle
=\langle a|h|a\rangle\langle a|b\rangle=0.}
$$
Likewise,
$$
\boxed{U=\frac{e^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0}
\iint\frac{|a(\mathbf r_1)|^2|a(\mathbf r_2)|^2}
{|\mathbf r_1-\mathbf r_2|}\,\mathrm d^3r_1\,\mathrm d^3r_2>0.}
$$
The integrand is nonnegative and is positive on a set of nonzero measure. This is the repulsion cost of <double occupancy>; the coincidence singularity is integrable for ordinary smooth localized orbitals.

**One <electron> on each center.** Suppress the normalized singlet <spin> factor and use $|\psi\rangle=C$. For each of the spatial products $ab$ and $ba$, the one-body sum has expectation $2E_0$. The cross terms vanish because a one-body hop leaves an orthogonal spectator orbital. The interaction contribution is the direct integral plus the <exchange integral>, both neglected in the model. Consequently
$$
\boxed{E_\psi=\langle C|H|C\rangle=2E_0.}
$$
This state has no same-center <double occupancy>, but remains an exchange-symmetric spatial superposition rather than assigning a distinguishable <electron> permanently to each <proton>.

**Two <electrons> in the bonding orbital.** The normalized one-electron bonding orbital is $g=(a+b)/\sqrt2$. The trial spatial state is $|\phi\rangle=|gg\rangle$, whose one-body expectation is
$$
2\langle g|h|g\rangle
=2\left[\frac{E_0+E_0+t+t}{2}\right]
=2E_0+2t.
$$
Expanding the product gives $|\phi\rangle=(|aa\rangle+|ab\rangle+|ba\rangle+|bb\rangle)/2$. The total <probability> of <double occupancy> is therefore $1/2$. Keeping only <on-site Coulomb repulsion> gives the intended interaction truncation,
$$
H_{1,2}\simeq U\bigl(|aa\rangle\langle aa|+|bb\rangle\langle bb|\bigr),
$$
and the interaction expectation is $U/2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{E_\phi=2E_0+2t+\frac U2.}
$$
This truncation must be stated. In particular, mixed Coulomb elements such as $X=\langle aa|H_{1,2}|ab\rangle$ do not vanish merely by orbital <orthogonality> or inversion symmetry. The printed list does not explicitly name them. If they are retained and are equal and real by site symmetry, their contribution to $E_\phi$ is $2X$; the requested expression uses their neglect along with the other off-site interaction terms. No numerical value of $X$ is supplied or invented.

**Energy comparison and the bond.** Put $x=|t|/U$ with $U>0$ and $t=-|t|$. Then
$$
\boxed{\frac{E_\phi-E_\psi}{U}=\frac12-2x.}
$$
The straight line starts at $1/2$ and crosses zero at $x=1/4$. The purely bonding-orbital trial state is better for $x>1/4$, while the singly occupied covalent trial state is better for $x<1/4$.

\Image[paper-1-two-site-bond.png]
{title=Original two-site singlet calculation: trial-energy comparison, continuous optimized energy and double-occupancy crossover in the stated on-site model}

For localized orbitals of approximately fixed atomic size, the hopping magnitude $|t|$ decreases rapidly, typically exponentially times a polynomial, as the <proton> separation grows. The on-site integral $U$ remains of atomic Coulomb scale and tends to the isolated-orbital value; it does not decay as the intersite repulsion $1/R$. If the orbitals themselves are allowed to relax, $U$ also changes, so no universal monotonic law for $U(R)$ follows from the data.

At small $|t|/U$, <electron correlation> suppresses the ionic configurations $aa$ and $bb$, favoring a singlet with roughly one <electron> on each center. A single doubly occupied bonding orbital has an artificial finite ionic weight even as the centers separate. At large $|t|/U$, delocalization gains enough <kinetic energy> that the bonding-orbital description becomes useful and charge fluctuations are substantial.

The crossing of two restricted trial energies is **not a sharp transition of the actual two-site <ground state>**. To see this without adding empirical data, include both $C$ and the symmetric ionic state $I_+=(|aa\rangle+|bb\rangle)/\sqrt2$. This <two-site Hubbard dimer> reduces to
$$
H-2E_0I=\begin{pmatrix}0&2t\\2t&U\end{pmatrix}_{C,I_+},
\qquad
\boxed{E_g=2E_0+\frac{U-\sqrt{U^2+16t^2}}2.}
$$
Its <ground state> is $\cos\theta\,C+\sin\theta\,I_+$, with $\tan2\theta=4|t|/U$ and $0\leq\theta\leq\pi/4$. The ionic <probability> is
$$
P_{\rm double}=\frac12\left(1-\frac{U}{\sqrt{U^2+16t^2}}\right).
$$
It changes smoothly from zero to $1/2$. At large separation, the electronic lowering is $-4t^2/U$ to leading order and vanishes as hopping vanishes. An absolute molecular binding curve would additionally require $E_0(R)$, the nuclear repulsion and the neglected intersite terms; this simplified comparison alone does not determine an equilibrium <bond length>.

= 4
{parent=paper-1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

**Uniform system and dispersion.** A <plane wave> reduces the differential <Hamiltonian> to a two-by-two <matrix> acting on its <spinor>:
$$
H(\mathbf k)=
\begin{pmatrix}
V&\hbar c(k_x-ik_y)\\
\hbar c(k_x+ik_y)&-V
\end{pmatrix}
=\mathbf d(\mathbf k)\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma,
\qquad
\mathbf d=(\hbar ck_x,\hbar ck_y,V).
$$
The Pauli anticommutation relations imply $H(\mathbf k)^2=|\mathbf d|^2I$. Choosing a <spinor> in one of its <eigenspaces> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{E_\pm(k)=\pm\sqrt{\hbar^2c^2k^2+V^2},\qquad k=|\mathbf k|.}
$$
An arbitrary constant <spinor> multiplying a <plane wave> is not necessarily an <energy eigenstate>; it must be an eigenvector of this <matrix>. For example, for the upper branch and $\varepsilon=|\mathbf d|$, one can use
$$
u_+(\mathbf k)=
\frac1{\sqrt{2\varepsilon(\varepsilon+V)}}
\begin{pmatrix}\varepsilon+V\\\hbar c(k_x+ik_y)\end{pmatrix},
$$
away from the coordinate singularity $\varepsilon+V=0$, where another normalized eigenvector chart is used.

For the usual $c>0$ convention, the massless branches are $E_\pm=\pm\hbar ck$. For nonzero $V$, the two branches have a gap $2|V|$ and are quadratic near $k=0$:
$$
E_\pm\simeq\pm|V|\pm\frac{\hbar^2c^2k^2}{2|V|}.
$$
**Changing the sign of $V$ does not change these energies.** It reverses the out-of-plane <spin> <polarization> of a given band at the origin. The plots for equal positive and negative mass magnitudes therefore have identical dispersion curves, despite their different <spinors>.

\Image[paper-1-dirac-bands.png]
{title=Original calculated massive and massless two-dimensional Dirac dispersions; equal and opposite masses share energies but reverse the band spin at zero momentum}

**<Spin> direction in the massless case.** For $k>0$ put $k_x+ik_y=ke^{i\varphi}$. The normalized eigenspinors can be chosen as
$$
u_s(\mathbf k)=\frac1{\sqrt2}
\begin{pmatrix}1\\s e^{i\varphi}\end{pmatrix},
\qquad E_s=s\hbar ck,\qquad s=\pm1.
$$
Direct multiplication gives $\langle\sigma_x\rangle=s\cos\varphi$, $\langle\sigma_y\rangle=s\sin\varphi$ and $\langle\sigma_z\rangle=0$. Since the <Pauli matrices> represent physical <spin> in this question,
$$
\boxed{\langle\mathbf S\rangle_s
=s\frac{\hbar}{2}\frac{(k_x,k_y,0)}{k}.}
$$
The <spin> is **parallel to <momentum> on the positive-energy branch and antiparallel on the negative-energy branch**. Thus the branch-independent statement is collinearity; the printed parallel claim needs the positive-branch qualification. At $k=0,V=0$, the <Hamiltonian> is zero and <spin> is not fixed by a <wavevector> direction.

**Mass-domain-wall <eigenstate>.** Assume $c>0$ for the stated normalizable wall state and write
$$
F(y)=\exp\!\left[-\frac1{\hbar c}\int_0^y V(s)\,\mathrm ds\right],
\qquad u_-=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Then $F'=-VF/(\hbar c)$, $\sigma_xu_-=-u_-$, and $(i\sigma_y+\sigma_z)u_-=0$. Applying the full operator gives
$$
H\{e^{ik_xx}F(y)u_-\}
=e^{ik_xx}F(y)
\{\hbar ck_x\sigma_x+iV(y)\sigma_y+V(y)\sigma_z\}u_-
=-\hbar ck_x\,e^{ik_xx}F(y)u_-.
$$
The transverse derivative and mass terms cancel pointwise. Hence **the specified state is an <eigenstate> with $E=-\hbar ck_x$**.

For a step mass $V(y)=V_+>0$ above the interface and $V(y)=V_-<0$ below it,
$$
F(y)=\begin{cases}
e^{-y/\xi_+},&y>0,\\
e^{y/\xi_-},&y<0,
\end{cases}
\qquad
\boxed{\xi_+=\frac{\hbar c}{V_+},\qquad
\xi_-=\frac{\hbar c}{|V_-|}.}
$$
The <localization length at a step Dirac mass wall> sets the two exponential tails. The amplitude is continuous, peaks at the interface and decays on both sides, with generally unequal decay lengths. The <probability density> decays with lengths $\xi_\pm/2$. A normalized transverse function is $f(y)=\sqrt{2/(\xi_++\xi_-)}F(y)$ when multiplying the unit <spinor> $u_-$. The longitudinal <plane wave> can independently be box-normalized. A step in $V$ allows a step in $F'$; the first-order Dirac equation does not impose continuity of that derivative.

\Image[paper-1-dirac-interface.png]
{title=Original mass-wall solution: asymmetric exponential confinement, the chiral energy branch and rigid longitudinal translation of a localized packet}

**Propagation.** The interface dispersion is the straight line
$$
\boxed{E(k_x)=-\hbar ck_x,\qquad
v_x=\frac1{\hbar}\frac{\mathrm dE}{\mathrm dk_x}=-c.}
$$
Every component on this branch travels in the negative $x$ direction. A packet has the form
$$
\Psi(x,y,t)=f(y)u_-\int A(k_x)e^{ik_x(x+ct)}\,\mathrm dk_x
=f(y)u_-\,g(x+ct).
$$
This <dispersionless chiral interface wavepacket> therefore translates rigidly with speed $c$ in that direction, without dispersion in this exactly linear model. It remains confined in $y$; there is no free transverse <group velocity>, because the bound-state family is not labelled by a transverse travelling-wave <momentum>.

The <continuity equation> gives the current components $j_x=c\Psi^\dagger\sigma_x\Psi=-c|\Psi|^2$ and $j_y=c\Psi^\dagger\sigma_y\Psi=0$. This explicitly confirms absence of <probability> transport away from the interface. For the step-mass case the profile is normalizable because its tails have positive decay constants and $c>0$. A general sign-changing profile also requires $F\in L^2(\mathbb R)$; nonzero asymptotic masses suffice, whereas signs alone do not guarantee confinement if their integrated magnitudes grow too slowly. If $c<0$, the supplied exponential instead grows at infinity; a normalizable state requires the opposite <spinor> and envelope sign. Reversing the wall orientation also reverses the propagation chirality.
